examPrepQuestion.modelAnswer:
1. Introduction & Philosophical Foundation:
Self-preservation is the first law of nature. The State cannot place a police officer at every citizen's elbow. Hence, Sections 34 to 44 of BNS 2023 (replacing Sections 96 to 106 IPC) recognize the Right of Private Defence. Section 34 declares: "Nothing is an offence which is done in the exercise of the right of private defence."

2. General Limitations (Section 37 BNS / Old Sec 99 IPC):
The right is not unrestricted and is subject to three crucial caveats:
(a) No right against a public servant acting in good faith under color of office.
(b) No right where there is reasonable time to seek recourse to public authorities.
(c) Proportionality: The right in no case extends to the inflicting of more harm than is necessary to inflict for the purpose of defence.

3. When Right of Defence of Body Extends to Causing Death (Section 38 BNS):
The right of private defence of body extends to voluntarily causing death or any other harm to the assailant if the offence occasions reasonable apprehension of:
(1) Death;
(2) Grievous hurt;
(3) Rape;
(4) Gratifying unnatural lust;
(5) Kidnapping or abduction;
(6) Wrongful confinement under circumstances which reasonably cause apprehension of inability to reach public authorities;
(7) Act of throwing or administering acid causing reasonable apprehension of grievous hurt.

4. When Right of Defence of Property Extends to Causing Death (Section 41 BNS):
The right of private defence of property extends to causing death only in 4 specific grave offences:
(1) Robbery;
(2) House-breaking after sunset and before sunrise;
(3) Mischief by fire or explosive substance committed on building, tent, or vessel used as human dwelling or custody of property;
(4) Theft, mischief, or house-trespass under circumstances causing reasonable apprehension that death or grievous hurt will be the consequence if private defence is not exercised.

5. Commencement and Continuance (Sections 39 & 42 BNS):
The right commences as soon as a reasonable apprehension of danger to body or property arises from an attempt or threat, and continues as long as such apprehension continues.

6. Judicial Principles (Darshan Singh Guidelines, 2010):
(i) Right is preventive and defensive, not punitive.
(ii) Cannot weigh defensive force in "golden scales" during the heat of the moment.
(iii) Accused need not prove private defence beyond reasonable doubt; preponderance of probability suffices.

7. Conclusion:
The right of private defence under BNS 2023 provides a complete statutory justification, protecting law-abiding citizens who resist unlawful violence within proportional boundaries.

